@startuml
' TUTORIAL 3 - Activity 6.
'
' The three rules the guide asks you to check:
'   (a) private attributes carry -, public methods carry +
'   (b) NOTHING points back up towards the controller
'   (c) the service's methods are listed with their return types
class Product {
  - id : Long
  - name : String
  - price : double
  + getId() : Long
  + getName() : String
  + getPrice() : double
  + setId(id : Long) : void
  + setName(name : String) : void
  + setPrice(price : double) : void
}
class ProductService {
  ' The angle brackets are PART OF THE TYPE and must be shown.
  ' Writing "- products : List" loses the information that makes
  ' the collection type-safe.
  - products : List<Product>
  - nextId : Long
  + getAllProducts() : List<Product>
  + getProductById(id : Long) : Optional<Product>
  + addProduct(product : Product) : Product
  + deleteProduct(id : Long) : void
  + findByName(name : String) : List<Product>
}
class ProductController {
  - productService : ProductService
  + getAllProducts() : List<Product>
  + getProductById(id : Long) : Product
  + addProduct(product : Product) : Product
  + deleteProduct(id : Long) : void
}
' ===== The two arrows =====
' ..>  DASHED line, open arrowhead - a DEPENDENCY.
' The controller uses the service but does not hold it as part of its
' own identity: give it a different service and it still works.
ProductController ..> ProductService
' -->  SOLID line, open arrowhead - an ASSOCIATION.
' The service HOLDS products. They are part of its state.
ProductService --> Product
' There is NO arrow from the service back to the controller. If you
' have drawn one, delete it - that is the mistake this activity exists
' to catch. The service does not know the web layer exists, which is
' exactly why it would run unchanged in a desktop application.
@enduml